the amazon workers votes now being  tallied will decide whether organized  labor can make a comeback in the heart  of the anti-union south the vote  counting that began on monday marks a  second battle between a corporate  behemoth and pro-union activists in  bessemer alabama employees rejected  joining the retail wholesale and  department store union last year but the  national labor relations board ruled  that amazon unfairly influenced the  outcome and called for a do-over  election that's in part because the  company set up a mailbox that looked  like a voting booth giving some workers  the impression amazon itself was running  the election we've turned a lot of no's  into yeses once they got the information  that was more impactful  to their lives so yeah they're on board  as well  the union's leader says the company's  tactics have continued  they are conducting a ferocious  anti-union campaign  union busting bringing in outside union  busters hundreds of them  in a statement amazon spokesperson kelly  nantel said we look forward to having  our employees voices heard our focus  remains on working directly with our  team to continue making amazon a great  place to work  the move in alabama follows a recent  vote still being counted at two amazon  sites on new york staten island where  workers projected their critiques of the  company under its iconic logo unions on  the decline for decades have seen a  resurgence in the past year  signing up workers at starbucks stores  in buffalo new york seattle and mesa  arizona google fiber contractors in  missouri and a new york city rei  sporting goods store  workers seem empowered by the great  resignation the wave of workers leaving  their jobs during the pandemic  the company has been calling workers in  for mandatory meetings to explain to  them why they should not unionize the  company's perspective is that it works  better with employees when it does so  directly and not through a third party  but many pro-union workers say when that  happens it's the company that sets the  rules  one alabama worker was confident enough  to take aim at amazon's billionaire  owner who also owns the blue origin  space company it's not really worth it  to stay with the company who  doesn't care about me as much as they  care about jeff bezos going to the moon  it could take weeks to count up to 6 100  ballots before it's clear if workers  here have opted to unionize and whether  organized labor can gain ground deep in  the heart of dixie john hendren al  jazeera bessemer alabama
 

